                <pb facs="0205" n="121" sameAs="frus:frus1903/pg121"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr. Conger.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-11-23"><hi rendition="#italic">November 23,
                        1901</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 416.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch No. 779, of the 16th ultimo, inclosing a copy of correspondence between
                    you and the Chinese foreign office upon the question of the continued residence
                    in Peking of foreign merchants who located there during the occupation of that
                    place by the allied forces.</p>
                <p>The Department approves your reply to the Chinese foreign office, as given in the
                    inclosure to the dispatch.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
